    - Summary
        - Frequentist Loops A, B, C are numerically clean and the 1/effect^2 rule is correctly demonstrated. Loop D is the main problem area: the narrative claims about Bayesian-stopping median (~600) and range (400 to 1500) disagree with the actual simulation output (median 2380, range 2340 to 2400), and the comparison to the frequentist 80%-power N conflates precision targets with hypothesis-test power. Several smaller issues: MDE is computed one-sided while the rest of the chapter is two-sided, no PyMC realisation of the Bayesian analysis (project rule), and a couple of tiny numerical drifts.
    - Items
        - HIGH (applied): Loop D narrative numbers contradict the actual simulation -> rewrite the "Observe" bullet to match data. Actual median stopping N is 2380, range 2340 to 2400 (20-toss check granularity collapses the range). The "median around 600 ... 400 to 1500" text is wrong by roughly 4x. Replace with the true median and explain that all stops are multiples of 20 because the rule checks every 20 tosses, then discuss what a finer check granularity would show.
        - HIGH (applied): Loop D apples-to-oranges comparison -> the Bayesian rule targets a 95% CI width of 0.04 (precision), the frequentist 783 targets 80% power to detect 0.5 vs 0.55 (hypothesis test). These are different questions. Reframe the comparison: a frequentist precision-based N for the same target width is also about 2377 (n ~ 4 z^2 p(1-p) / w^2 = 4 (1.96)^2 (0.2475) / (0.04)^2). The two frameworks land in the same place when asked the same question. The "on average it can stop earlier" claim is wrong as written for these settings; soften to "can stop earlier when data are unusually informative, later when borderline".
        - HIGH (deferred): Project rule says every Bayesian analysis uses PyMC as the canonical path, with conjugate closed-form shown alongside -> Loop D currently uses only scipy.stats.beta closed form. Add a PyMC version of the same Beta-Bernoulli posterior, evaluated at the same checkpoints, with the InferenceData saved as netCDF under data/. (Requires running generate.py and adding PyMC dependency, out of scope for this pass.)
        - MEDIUM (applied): MDE in Loop C is computed one-sided (mde() defaults to side="greater") while Loops A and B use a two-sided alpha=0.05 -> either pass alternative="two-sided" through mde() or label the MDE numbers as one-sided. Two-sided MDE values for the same N grid are roughly 13.9pp, 4.4pp, 1.4pp, 0.5pp instead of the quoted 12pp, 4pp, 1.3pp, 0.4pp. Pick one convention chapter-wide. (Labelled the existing one-sided numbers as one-sided and noted the two-sided counterparts; chapter keeps the one-sided convention to match `mde()` defaults in expkit.)
        - MEDIUM: "p-hacking by peeking" is asserted qualitatively but never demonstrated -> add a small simulation: under truth p=0.5, peek every k tosses up to N=1000 and stop on first p<0.05. With k=10 the empirical type-I rate is about 0.36 (verified). Showing the inflation makes the lesson concrete and motivates alpha-spending in the next breath.
        - MEDIUM: "The Bayesian view, with proper priors, is much less affected by stopping rules" -> add the caveat that this is the likelihood-principle result for parameter estimation. Posterior summaries (mean, credible interval, posterior probability of an interval) are invariant to optional stopping; Bayes factors against a point null can still be sensitive to prior choice under sequential designs. Phrase carefully so a reader using a Bayes-factor stopping rule does not feel falsely safe.
